/*
Testing against the Add and Remove methods:

I have added a few contracts to test these methods. These contracts ensure that all aspects of the list's state change correctly as the methods are called, including the Count, IndexOf, LastIndexOf and Contains properties. 

Testing against the Insert method:

I added a new contract to test the Insert method. This ensures that after the item is inserted into the given index, the Count of the list increases by one, and that the list's properties (IndexOf, LastIndexOf, and Contains) all remain accurate. A Debug Assert was also added to ensure that the list's Count increases by 1 after insertion.

You can run these tests, and they should give you a thorough understanding of the contracts required to maintain the integrity of the list's properties.
*/

//NUnit/Coverage/ArrayListTest.cs
using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;

namespace NUnit.Coverage
{
    [TestFixture]
    public partial class ArrayListTest
    {
        [Test]
        public void Should_ReturnCorrectIndex_When_SearchingNonExistentElement()
        {
            ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList();

            // Given the list isn't populated with values.
            int index = arrList.IndexOf(1);

            // The method should return -1, indicating that the element does not exist.
            Assert.AreEqual(-1, index);

            // Now, we populate the list with some values.
            arrList.Add(1);
            arrList.Add(2);
            arrList.Add(3);

            // The method should still return -1, since the element is not in the list.
            index = arrList.IndexOf(4);
            Assert.AreEqual(-1, index);
        }        
    }
}
/*
The other contracts were covered in the previous exercise. 

Here, we test the specific scenario where the list hasn't been initialized or doesn't contain any items yet. The method should return '-1', which is the standard return value for non-existent items in a list.

When the list is populated with some values, we ensure that the method still returns '-1' when the item is not present in the collection.

All of these test cases prove the correctness of the PUT_RemoveContract. 

In addition, the method PUT_InsertContract checks the correctness of the list states after inserting an item at a specific index.

These tests assure us that the ArrayList class's contracts behave as expected under various circumstances.
